WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.894
The first A3 lesson becomes much easier once the learner stops imagining two waves politely taking turns at one point.

2
00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:11.788
In wave physics, overlap is simultaneous.

3
00:00:11.788 --> 00:00:17.682
If two progressive waves meet at the same place and time, the displacement there must be read from both contributions together.

4
00:00:17.682 --> 00:00:23.575
That is the superposition rule.

5
00:00:23.575 --> 00:00:29.469
The combined result may reinforce, partly cancel, or fully cancel depending on phase.

6
00:00:29.469 --> 00:00:35.363
But the important idea comes first.

7
00:00:35.363 --> 00:00:41.257
The overlap point has one displacement at that instant, and that displacement is built by adding the separate contributions.

8
00:00:41.257 --> 00:00:47.151
Once that rule is trusted, interference ideas later in the module stop feeling like unrelated tricks.

9
00:00:47.151 --> 00:00:53.045
Keep this takeaway in view: Superposition begins with one question: what are both displacements at this point right now?

10
00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:58.938
Wave type means progressive.

11
00:00:58.938 --> 00:01:04.832
The wave travels and carries energy while the pattern moves through the medium.

12
00:01:04.832 --> 00:01:10.726
Core rule means add displacement.

13
00:01:10.726 --> 00:01:16.620
The result at one point comes from both wave contributions at the same instant.

14
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:22.817
This diagram is valuable because it stops the learner from talking vaguely about two waves meeting.

15
00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:29.013
Instead, it makes the overlap measurable.

16
00:01:29.013 --> 00:01:35.210
At one location, each incoming wave contributes its own displacement.

17
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:41.407
The combined wave shape at that location is the sum of those contributions.

18
00:01:41.407 --> 00:01:47.603
If both displacements point the same way, the overlap reinforces.

19
00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:53.800
If they oppose each other, the overlap shrinks or cancels.

20
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:59.997
The diagram therefore does more than decorate the lesson.

21
00:01:59.997 --> 00:02:06.193
It shows why superposition is an addition rule.

22
00:02:06.193 --> 00:02:12.390
The eye can track separate contributions and then see the combined outcome without pretending that one wave disappears while the other takes over.

23
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:18.587
Keep this takeaway in view: A clear overlap diagram makes superposition feel like a rule of reading rather than a fact to memorize.

24
00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:24.783
Input one: The first progressive wave contributes its own displacement at the chosen point.

25
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:30.980
Input two: The second progressive wave contributes at that same point and time.

26
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.894
A helpful analogy is two people pushing one swing at the same moment.

27
00:02:35.894 --> 00:02:40.809
If both pushes act in the same direction at that instant, the swing gets a stronger overall push.

28
00:02:40.809 --> 00:02:45.723
If one push opposes the other, the net effect is smaller.

29
00:02:45.723 --> 00:02:50.637
The swing does not experience one push first and the other later if they are simultaneous.

30
00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:55.551
It responds to the combined effect.

31
00:02:55.551 --> 00:03:00.466
That is a good bridge into superposition.

32
00:03:00.466 --> 00:03:05.380
The overlap point on the wave behaves like the swing.

33
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:10.294
It does not store two separate outcomes.

34
00:03:10.294 --> 00:03:15.209
It has one resulting displacement that reflects the sum of the separate contributions at that instant.

35
00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:20.123
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy works only if the pushes are read as simultaneous, just like the wave contributions.

36
00:03:20.123 --> 00:03:25.037
Same direction means reinforce.

37
00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:29.951
Two matching contributions build a larger instant displacement.

38
00:03:29.951 --> 00:03:34.866
Opposite direction means cancel.

39
00:03:34.866 --> 00:03:39.780
Opposing contributions reduce the result or can cancel it completely.

40
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.400
The central relationship is simple but powerful.

41
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.020
Resultant displacement equals the first displacement plus the second displacement.

42
00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.640
That is the heart of superposition.

43
00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.260
If the contributions are in phase, the result becomes larger.

44
00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.880
If they are opposite in phase, the result becomes smaller or zero.

45
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:13.500
The key is that the addition is algebraic, so sign and direction matter.

46
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.120
The formula therefore tells the learner how to read the overlap point.

47
00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.740
First identify both contributions.

48
00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:30.360
Then add them with the correct sign.

49
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.980
Only after that should the learner describe the result as reinforcement or cancellation.

50
00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:41.600
Keep this takeaway in view: Superposition works when the learner reads one point, one instant, and then one algebraic sum.

51
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:47.220
Positive and negative: The sign of each displacement matters because the sum is algebraic, not just size-only.

52
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:52.840
Reinforcement: A larger result appears when the contributions act together at that instant.

53
00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:58.460
resultant displacement = first displacement + second displacement.

54
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:04.080
in phase contributions give reinforcement.

55
00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.700
The equation is strongest when it is read as one-point wave bookkeeping rather than as a detached symbol line.

56
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:16.210
A strong routine is to freeze one point and one instant first.

57
00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:22.720
Then read the displacement from wave one and the displacement from wave two.

58
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:29.230
Then add the two algebraically and only after that decide whether the overlap is reinforcement, partial cancellation, or full cancellation.

59
00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:35.740
This routine matters because learners often jump straight to naming the outcome without first reading the contributions.

60
00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:42.250
The safer route is contribution first, result second.

61
00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:48.760
Once that habit is strong, later path-difference and phase stories feel much more controlled.

62
00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:55.270
Keep this takeaway in view: The same-place, same-time routine is the best protection against shallow overlap language.

63
00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:01.780
Step 1 means freeze the point.

64
00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:08.290
Choose one location and one instant before reading any wave contribution.

65
00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:14.800
Step 2 means read both inputs.

66
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:21.310
Identify the displacement contributed by each wave at that same moment.

67
00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:25.263
Try four checks.

68
00:06:25.263 --> 00:06:29.217
During overlap, do waves use the point one after another or together?

69
00:06:29.217 --> 00:06:33.170
Together.

70
00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:37.123
Is the key quantity route length or displacement at one instant?

71
00:06:37.123 --> 00:06:41.077
Displacement at one instant.

72
00:06:41.077 --> 00:06:45.030
If two equal contributions act in opposite directions, does the result shrink?

73
00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:48.983
Yes.

74
00:06:48.983 --> 00:06:52.937
And if two equal contributions act together in the same direction, does the result grow?

75
00:06:52.937 --> 00:06:56.890
Yes.

76
00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:00.843
Those answers show that the learner is reading superposition as a same-place, same-time addition rule.

77
00:07:00.843 --> 00:07:04.797
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can defend overlap as algebraic addition at one point and time.

78
00:07:04.797 --> 00:07:08.750
Overlap means simultaneous.

79
00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:12.703
The point experiences both contributions at the same time.

80
00:07:12.703 --> 00:07:16.657
Quantity means displacement.

81
00:07:16.657 --> 00:07:20.610
The rule applies to displacement at one point, not to route length or travel history.

82
00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:26.390
You are ready for the worked example now.

83
00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:32.170
Begin by selecting the point and instant being discussed.

84
00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:37.950
Then read the displacement contributed by each wave there.

85
00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:43.730
Add the values with the correct sign.

86
00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:49.510
Only after that should you describe the result as reinforcement or cancellation.

87
00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:55.290
The strongest answer is the one that proves how the combined displacement was formed, not the one that jumps to a label without showing the overlap logic.

88
00:07:55.290 --> 00:08:01.070
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is easiest when superposition is treated as disciplined overlap bookkeeping.

89
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:06.850
Do not rush: A quick label without reading both contributions usually hides the real physics.

90
00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:12.630
Keep the sign: The sum must respect direction because opposite displacements reduce the result.

91
00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:18.410
freeze one point and one instant.

92
00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:24.190
read displacement from each overlapping wave.

93
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:29.970
A strong solution shows the input contributions before it names the final overlap result.
